WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:01.349
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:01.349 --> 00:00:03.669
Today's message is push through.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.879
Over to you, Mike Rashid.

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:08.100
(rooster crowing)

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:08.933
(energetic rhythmic music)

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:10.899
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:10.899 --> 00:00:13.608
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:17.531
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:17.531 --> 00:00:18.811
- There's different types
of people in this world

00:00:18.811 --> 00:00:21.760
And one thing that we're
all going to deal with

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.050
is like difficulties in our tasks,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.960
trying to become successful.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.379
As good as things get, you're
going to have the same amount

00:00:29.379 --> 00:00:32.277
of bad that's going to try
to impede your progress,

00:00:32.277 --> 00:00:33.971
whether it be people,
whether it be the IRS,

00:00:33.971 --> 00:00:38.829
whether it be whatever,
something will rear its ugly head

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:40.371
and try to block you.

00:00:40.371 --> 00:00:43.819
Now a lot of people
can't handle their shit.

00:00:43.819 --> 00:00:47.442
When you have a vision
and something or someone

00:00:47.442 --> 00:00:51.138
throws a curve ball, it
hurts, it stings, right?

00:00:51.138 --> 00:00:55.319
It's like your baby and now
I can't put my baby out there

00:00:55.319 --> 00:00:58.230
like I want to do it, so it
really throws people off,

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.890
they get real negative, they
make (mumbles) want to stop,

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.379
they might just want to go
back to their nine to five.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:06.430
That's fine, it's not
for everybody to pursue,

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.824
but for those who can
pursue, like you said,

00:01:08.824 --> 00:01:10.795
the difference between
those who really succeed

00:01:10.795 --> 00:01:13.591
and those who don't, are the ones who,

00:01:13.591 --> 00:01:17.533
when they're faced with
whatever these difficulties are,

00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:18.401
they're like (mumbles) it,

00:01:18.401 --> 00:01:19.913
I'm going to keep on pushing through,

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:20.962
I'm going to keep pushing through.

00:01:20.962 --> 00:01:23.778
Because it sound corny and cliche,

00:01:23.778 --> 00:01:25.348
but there's always
sunshine after the rain.

00:01:25.348 --> 00:01:28.382
Rain don't last that long,
you got to know that,

00:01:28.382 --> 00:01:29.831
be aware of that.

00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:31.142
That's something you
can take with you during

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:34.720
everything in life, but in
business you can't not stop

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.522
because things are not
going right at the time

00:01:36.522 --> 00:01:37.955
because it's going to happen.

00:01:37.955 --> 00:01:40.962
I dealt with it, I'm sure I'll
deal with it again sometime.

00:01:40.962 --> 00:01:42.825
- Starting a business is
going to be one of the hardest

00:01:42.825 --> 00:01:45.551
things you'll ever do
in your life, period.

00:01:45.551 --> 00:01:47.434
It just is, if you think
starting a business is going to

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:51.025
be easy, you are so disillusioned and

00:01:51.025 --> 00:01:52.674
you're setting yourself up for failure.

00:01:52.674 --> 00:01:55.775
You may have an easy idea,
you may think it's so simple,

00:01:55.775 --> 00:01:59.065
but the execution of it,
building up momentum,

00:01:59.065 --> 00:02:01.858
getting customers, getting
this thing out there,

00:02:01.858 --> 00:02:05.484
adding value, it's hard, it's
going to be one of the hardest

00:02:05.484 --> 00:02:08.649
things, if not, the hardest
thing, that you ever do

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:09.673
in your life.

00:02:09.673 --> 00:02:12.489
And it's totally worth it,
and it's totally rewarding,

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:14.152
but I want to set up
your expectations because

00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:16.683
as fun as entrepreneurship is,

00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:18.371
and how great it is to
be your own boss and

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:19.789
set your own hours, and
do something meaningful,

00:02:19.789 --> 00:02:21.823
and have an impact, and
make way more money,

00:02:21.823 --> 00:02:24.842
as awesome as that is, it's also a grind,

00:02:24.842 --> 00:02:29.009
and a battle, and constantly
daily fighting self worth

00:02:30.697 --> 00:02:33.340
when your thing, your
idea, doesn't take off

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.932
and doesn't work immediately.

00:02:34.932 --> 00:02:36.122
It's hard.

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.462
When I was in the
venture capital business,

00:02:37.462 --> 00:02:41.181
we'd often challenge the
people coming to raise capital

00:02:41.181 --> 00:02:42.710
to give us three scenarios,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.811
your realistic case scenario,
your worst case scenario,

00:02:45.811 --> 00:02:46.951
and your best case scenario.

00:02:46.951 --> 00:02:49.508
If we give you a million
dollars for you company,

00:02:49.508 --> 00:02:52.289
give us the three
scenarios, best, realistic,

00:02:52.289 --> 00:02:53.839
worst case scenario.

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:55.290
How does that money come back to us?

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.448
What does that money
look like in the growth

00:02:56.448 --> 00:02:57.281
of your business?

00:02:57.281 --> 00:03:00.758
We would always only look
at the worst case scenario,

00:03:00.758 --> 00:03:03.481
and then imagine it's going
to be worse than that.

00:03:03.481 --> 00:03:06.550
So if we're not making money
off the worst case scenario,

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.010
then forget about it.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.885
And if we're barely making
money off the worst case

00:03:09.885 --> 00:03:11.551
scenario, then probably
forget about it too

00:03:11.551 --> 00:03:13.664
because it's likely going
to be way worse than

00:03:13.664 --> 00:03:14.833
the worst case scenario.

00:03:14.833 --> 00:03:17.434
And entrepreneurs are
optimistic, and it's great,

00:03:17.434 --> 00:03:18.305
and you need to be optimistic.

00:03:18.305 --> 00:03:20.862
And I think that's the only
thing that kind of keeps

00:03:20.862 --> 00:03:24.432
you sane when it's so crappy around you,

00:03:24.432 --> 00:03:26.862
when you're just fighting
battles all day long,

00:03:26.862 --> 00:03:28.853
it's that optimism that gets you through,

00:03:28.853 --> 00:03:30.464
it's that passion for what
you're doing that gets you

00:03:30.464 --> 00:03:32.231
through, it's the belief
that something good is

00:03:32.231 --> 00:03:35.032
going to come from this when
you're fighting in the trenches

00:03:35.032 --> 00:03:38.373
that gets you through all
the garbage, all the crap

00:03:38.373 --> 00:03:40.503
of working because
otherwise you would quit

00:03:40.503 --> 00:03:41.914
and go do something else.

00:03:41.914 --> 00:03:43.771
The thing that gets you
through is just a mad love

00:03:43.771 --> 00:03:45.664
for what you're doing,
because often, at the start,

00:03:45.664 --> 00:03:47.603
it doesn't make logical practical sense.

00:03:47.603 --> 00:03:49.774
You continue doing it
just because you love it.

00:03:49.774 --> 00:03:52.911
I look at this, this career
that I've built for myself,

00:03:52.911 --> 00:03:53.744
and what I'm doing.

00:03:53.744 --> 00:03:55.831
One of my very first speaking engagements,

00:03:55.831 --> 00:03:59.671
my first one in Canada, was at the YMCA.

00:03:59.671 --> 00:04:01.303
(soft inspiring music)

00:04:01.303 --> 00:04:05.462
And I went to this room,
and they had this free event

00:04:05.462 --> 00:04:07.073
that we were both promoting.

00:04:07.073 --> 00:04:08.372
They had entrepreneurship program,

00:04:08.372 --> 00:04:10.251
and I had some people following me,

00:04:10.251 --> 00:04:12.849
and we promoted it, this
free event, show up,

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:16.599
in this big room, and
three people showed up.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:21.270
Three people showed up a free event!

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:22.729
Where there was all ready an audience.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:24.647
And so you could look at
that experience and say

00:04:24.647 --> 00:04:27.219
well if you could only get
three people to show up

00:04:27.219 --> 00:04:28.691
for a free thing, how are
you ever going to make this

00:04:28.691 --> 00:04:30.101
into a business?

00:04:30.101 --> 00:04:31.809
It doesn't make any sense.

00:04:31.809 --> 00:04:33.518
And when I first walked into
the room and I saw three

00:04:33.518 --> 00:04:35.910
people, my heart sunk.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.907
I felt terrible, I felt
worthless, I felt this was a

00:04:38.907 --> 00:04:40.020
big waste of time.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.478
Like, why did I spend all
this time for preparing

00:04:42.478 --> 00:04:45.390
all these slides and this
presentation for so long

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.520
for three people.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.528
And I quickly kind of just
suppressed that to say you know

00:04:50.528 --> 00:04:52.730
what, three people showed
up, I all ready did the work,

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.488
let's at least give some
value to these three people.

00:04:55.488 --> 00:04:57.071
And it was amazing.

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:01.670
Like it just, it nourished my soul.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.968
I felt so good going
through the content and

00:05:03.968 --> 00:05:06.628
watching these three people
come alive and realize

00:05:06.628 --> 00:05:10.238
that they now had some hope
and they had a belief in

00:05:10.238 --> 00:05:12.919
themselves that they would
go home and now, hopefully,

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:14.838
bring this to their business
and see a big change

00:05:14.838 --> 00:05:16.237
in their company and their life.

00:05:16.237 --> 00:05:19.250
That felt so good that I
could give that to them.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:22.379
And walking into that room initially,

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:24.800
I'm thinking this could
never be a business.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.160
And I'm thinking just from
a practical point of view,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.738
and I'm walking out of that
room at the end thinking,

00:05:30.738 --> 00:05:32.907
I have to find a way to keep going.

00:05:32.907 --> 00:05:34.439
Even if this never becomes a business,

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:36.218
even if I never make money off of this,

00:05:36.218 --> 00:05:39.170
it was such a good high
just knowing that I helped

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.968
these people, that even if
I could never find a way

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:44.586
to make money off of this,
I need to keep doing it

00:05:44.586 --> 00:05:47.886
because it fills me, it nourishes me.

00:05:47.886 --> 00:05:51.557
And it's because of that
that I kept going through

00:05:51.557 --> 00:05:54.157
all the garbage, through all the crap,

00:05:54.157 --> 00:05:57.270
through all the fights
and battles, and struggle,

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.149
to get to where I'm at now.

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:01.482
The practice, the experience, the grind,

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.320
is because it fills me up,
and I eventually found a way

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.997
to make money, I found a way
to make it become my career.

00:06:10.997 --> 00:06:13.469
And this is going to be
the same thing for you.

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:16.719
At the start, you have a business plan.

00:06:17.648 --> 00:06:20.380
The business plan quickly
falls apart as you start

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.278
actually getting into
business, you'll realize this.

00:06:22.278 --> 00:06:24.101
No matter how great a plan you have,

00:06:24.101 --> 00:06:27.033
what actually happens is no
where close to the business plan

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:28.867
and you need to adjust.

00:06:28.867 --> 00:06:32.637
And every practical,
logical, sane, safe reason

00:06:32.637 --> 00:06:35.967
in the world would tell you to stop.

00:06:35.967 --> 00:06:38.396
This isn't working, you're
putting time, you're putting

00:06:38.396 --> 00:06:40.295
money, you're putting
energy into this thing

00:06:40.295 --> 00:06:42.207
and it's not working.

00:06:42.207 --> 00:06:45.846
But the absolute love for the
craft and what you're doing,

00:06:45.846 --> 00:06:48.476
and how it makes you
feel, keeps pushing you in

00:06:48.476 --> 00:06:50.476
that direction to keep going.

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:53.668
And if you do that enough, you
keep pushing through enough,

00:06:53.668 --> 00:06:55.677
you keep loving what you're doing enough,

00:06:55.677 --> 00:06:59.438
you keep getting better,
you'll find a way to add value

00:06:59.438 --> 00:07:02.067
to the point where you're
actually helping customers,

00:07:02.067 --> 00:07:03.729
and you can turn it into a business.

00:07:03.729 --> 00:07:07.646
Most people don't have
the love to push through

00:07:09.156 --> 00:07:13.323
all the crap to turn their
dream into something amazing.

00:07:14.509 --> 00:07:17.138
So I'm challenging you to
do it today with yours.

00:07:17.138 --> 00:07:19.035
So the question today is I'm curious,

00:07:19.035 --> 00:07:21.298
what's the hardest thing
you've had to push through

00:07:21.298 --> 00:07:22.715
in your business?

00:07:23.918 --> 00:07:24.751
Leave it in the comments below,

00:07:24.751 --> 00:07:25.584
we're going to join in the discussion.

00:07:25.584 --> 00:07:27.599
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Sean Terry.

00:07:27.599 --> 00:07:29.974
Sean, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:07:29.974 --> 00:07:32.835
Your One Word, it really,
really, really means a lot to me.

00:07:32.835 --> 00:07:34.417
And I hope you're enjoying it.

00:07:34.417 --> 00:07:36.884
Thank you guys so much for
watching, I believe in you,

00:07:36.884 --> 00:07:38.548
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:07:38.548 --> 00:07:41.067
and whatever your one word is,

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:42.306
and I'll see you again
tomorrow morning for another

00:07:42.306 --> 00:07:44.176
shot of espresso.

00:07:44.176 --> 00:07:47.688
(fun rhythmic music)
♫ I wake up every morning

00:07:47.688 --> 00:07:50.857
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:07:50.857 --> 00:07:52.610
Hey Believe Nation, thank
you so much for watching.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:55.240
If you want to learn
more about this subject,

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.795
get a little bit deeper on
it, I'm going to show you

00:07:57.795 --> 00:08:01.795
some extra bonus clips
that I hope you'll enjoy.

00:08:12.588 --> 00:08:16.226
- All the time, all the time,
and then you push through it.

00:08:16.226 --> 00:08:17.977
Like you push through it
because what's the alternative?

00:08:17.977 --> 00:08:20.953
You're going to leave that idea
stuck in your head forever.

00:08:20.953 --> 00:08:21.870
That sucks.

00:08:23.364 --> 00:08:25.625
The alternative is you go through life,

00:08:25.625 --> 00:08:27.235
and you have this great
idea, and nothing came of it

00:08:27.235 --> 00:08:29.754
because you got tired.

00:08:29.754 --> 00:08:33.176
And yeah, sometimes you
don't go to the party that

00:08:33.176 --> 00:08:37.343
all your friends are at
because the idea is calling

00:08:38.296 --> 00:08:39.347
to you in that moment.

00:08:39.347 --> 00:08:40.554
Wait for it's a great example of that.

00:08:40.554 --> 00:08:43.056
I was on my way to my
friends birthday party

00:08:43.056 --> 00:08:45.995
when that lyric showed up,
when death doesn't discriminate

00:08:45.995 --> 00:08:47.097
showed up in my head.

00:08:47.097 --> 00:08:50.864
The idea came on the A down,
I started writing it down

00:08:50.864 --> 00:08:53.514
in the train (mumbles) of the A train.

00:08:53.514 --> 00:08:57.154
Plate dancers are dancing,
I have my headphones on.

00:08:57.154 --> 00:09:01.071
I get out, the whole
chorus comes, all at once.

00:09:01.913 --> 00:09:05.652
I write it down, and I
suddenly see the shape of

00:09:05.652 --> 00:09:08.829
the whole song, I was
like the chorus Izbur,

00:09:08.829 --> 00:09:10.147
that's who he is.

00:09:10.147 --> 00:09:14.314
I go into my friends party,
I go, "Hey, what's up man?

00:09:15.701 --> 00:09:17.709
"Happy birthday, I got to go."

00:09:17.709 --> 00:09:19.529
(audience laughing)

00:09:19.529 --> 00:09:22.129
I get on the L train and I
write the rest of the song

00:09:22.129 --> 00:09:23.836
on the way back home, from the L to the A,

00:09:23.836 --> 00:09:25.668
back up to 207th street.

00:09:25.668 --> 00:09:28.456
You have to do that sometimes,
you have to say no to

00:09:28.456 --> 00:09:30.858
your friends to say yes to your work.

00:09:30.858 --> 00:09:31.848
Because what are you going to do?

00:09:31.848 --> 00:09:35.036
Like lose that idea
because you decided to have

00:09:35.036 --> 00:09:37.270
a drink with your friends,
it's not worth it!

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.629
Your friend will be there.

00:09:38.629 --> 00:09:41.139
- [Man] You also went to
Broadway with Julia Roberts,

00:09:41.139 --> 00:09:42.890
with Paul Rudd, Three Days of Rain.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:44.100
That must have been pretty special.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.579
- Yeah, it was a do or die
moment for me actually.

00:09:48.579 --> 00:09:52.496
(mumbles) had gotten canceled,

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.038
and I remembered thinking
if this doesn't work,

00:09:58.038 --> 00:10:00.101
maybe I'm not right for
this business, I thought.

00:10:00.101 --> 00:10:01.870
There are moments in life
when you think, you know what,

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:06.569
it kind of does matter if
this fails, or you fail in it.

00:10:06.569 --> 00:10:10.129
And it was hard, it was
the hardest thing I've ever

00:10:10.129 --> 00:10:12.639
been through, by far.

00:10:12.639 --> 00:10:15.289
And I remembered thinking
if I can get through this,

00:10:15.289 --> 00:10:18.635
it will maybe as hard,
but it's impossible for it

00:10:18.635 --> 00:10:20.047
to be harder, and it's true.

00:10:20.047 --> 00:10:22.476
And I've had a lot of
hard times since then,

00:10:22.476 --> 00:10:23.837
more jobs, you know, when you're like wow,

00:10:23.837 --> 00:10:26.009
the pressure's so
intense, but it always...

00:10:26.009 --> 00:10:28.229
I have such a foundation
from Three Days of Rain.

00:10:28.229 --> 00:10:29.889
- [Man] I was going to say,
eight shows a week, right?

00:10:29.889 --> 00:10:32.188
- Not even that, it actually
was the rehearsal process,

00:10:32.188 --> 00:10:35.639
once we got on stage,
it flew, and thank god.

00:10:35.639 --> 00:10:36.986
And I thought, oh, this feels right.

00:10:36.986 --> 00:10:38.208
But it was a...

00:10:38.208 --> 00:10:41.274
And you know, I was hard
on myself, but it was just,

00:10:41.274 --> 00:10:42.705
man, it was tough.

00:10:42.705 --> 00:10:45.408
- See, I'm a seed, I really am.

00:10:45.408 --> 00:10:48.241
See, but a seed has to be planted,

00:10:49.518 --> 00:10:53.338
a seed got to have dirt put on top of it.

00:10:53.338 --> 00:10:55.750
If you take a seed and
throw it on the concrete,

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:59.285
and walk off, the sun
will just burn it up.

00:10:59.285 --> 00:11:02.368
But guess what, logically in my mind,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.190
it doesn't make sense
that to grow something,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:11.420
you should dig a hole,
put it down in there,

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:12.970
and cover it with dirt.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.878
Logically that don't make no sense to me.

00:11:15.878 --> 00:11:19.795
But oh though, see dirt
is necessary for growth

00:11:22.438 --> 00:11:26.478
and development, dirt builds character,

